      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Tear the flatbreads into rough 5cm pieces. Toss with 2 tbsp olive oil and spread on a baking tray. Toast at 200C for 8 to 10 minutes until golden and crisp."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat 1 tbsp oil in a saucepan. Add canned tomatoes, cumin, allspice, and half the salt. Cook for 5 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add chickpeas and 100ml water. Simmer for 10 minutes until the chickpeas are hot and have absorbed some of the sauce."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mix yoghurt with crushed garlic, lemon juice, and remaining salt in a bowl. Keep cold."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "To assemble: spread the toasted bread pieces across a large serving dish. Spoon the hot chickpea mixture over."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pour the cold yoghurt sauce over the chickpeas. Scatter with toasted cashews, parsley, and a pinch of sweet paprika. Serve immediately."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Bread goes soggy before serving",
              "cause": "It was assembled too far in advance.",
              "fix": "Assemble at the very last moment. The bread should be crisp when you pour the hot chickpeas and cold yoghurt over."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Yoghurt sauce is too thick",
              "cause": "Thick yoghurt used without loosening.",
              "fix": "Stir in 2 to 3 tablespoons of water and beat until smooth and pourable."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Fatteh is eaten across Lebanon, Syria, Jordan, and Egypt, with each country having its own variation. The Lebanese version most commonly uses chickpeas. The Egyptian version uses lamb or chicken. The word fatteh comes from the Arabic root for crumbling or tearing, which describes what happens to the bread as it softens under the warm toppings. In Lebanon it is a festive morning dish, often served at Ramadan suhoor before the fast begins. It is also a popular cold-weather breakfast in Beirut's old neighbourhoods."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
